  You will have problems, as you will have to deal with the VMS$COMMON
  portion of the file structure -- these are the shared files for the
  OpenVMS system.  Each operting system will have its own VMS$COMMON
  contents, hence you will have a problem if you want multiple OpenVMS
  versions coresident on the same disk.

:The manual refers to booting from [SYSF]. I do not have all  the manuals
:specific for this model Vax. Does anyone know the proper command???

  >>> b/r5:f0000000

  But if you are asking this question, you probably don't want to
  try what you are considering.  (Without intending to sound rude,
  it would appear you are inexperienced -- what you are attempting
  involves some knowledge of the system disk file structure and it
  means you will be creating a configuration that is technically 
  considered unsupported.  This is not to say it cannot be done.)

  A more appropriate (supported) approach would involve booting from
  a second system disk.  (DOS consoles can deal primarily with a
  single disk and thus DOS "likes" partitions and multiple operating
  systems in single partitions, while OpenVMS and its consoles can 
  and do prefer to deal with multiple system disks for this purpose.)

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




