Robert Young wrote:
> 
> Does anyone have a good, concise description of how to perform a rolling
> VMS upgrade on a 2 node cluster? I do not have the VMS Cluster systems
> for OpenVMS manual and need help in figuring out how to handle the
> expected votes and quorum disk settings, and how to change them on a Vax
> and an AXP system.

If you have a quorum disk, it is straightforward. If you don't it is 
difficult. (You have to manually unlock the lost-quorum state on one
machine when you shut down the other.)

In case you don't already know, a quorum disk is a voting disk which
allows 
a 2-node cluster to shut one node down. The machines have a vote each,
the
disk has a vote, expected_votes=3, quorum=2 (which of those is in use 
depends on the VMS version), and losing any one node (including the disk 
here as a potential loss) doesn't lock up the remaining systems.

A quorum disk is a disk which is directly reachable by either machine,
i.e.
it should not be local to one machine and served to the other, as in
that
case loss of the server machine loses the disk as well, and the
remaining 
machine locks up.

The disk is also restricted in some other ways. I am not sure of
details, 
but I suspect it cannot be part of a volume or shadow set, and it is
recommended that it is not a system disk.

So, if you have an interconnect between the machines which holds disks,
e.g. CI, SCSI etc, then you can designate one disk as a quorum disk.
Then you can shut down one machine to do the upgrade without preventing
use of the other. Then you just have to consider whether local disks on
one machine are used by the other machine.

You should also probably copy SYSUAF.DAT and RIGHTSLIST.DAT from your 
common location (if you have one) into SYS$SYSTEM on each machine
before doing the standalone reboot for upgrade. That way you will be
able to log in with a password you know. It is not required for the 
VMS upgrade itself but it does allow you to work on the machine in 
standalone mode afterwards.

Obviously you need to make sure that the two versions of VMS you will 
be running during the rolling upgrade are compatable. Usually one
version different is OK, and several specific larger steps are
supported.

From memory, I believe you can run 5.5-2 and 6.1 together, 6.2 and 7.1
and some other combinations. Check the upgrade documentation for this.

Have fun...

-- 
Alan Adams



